# runloop_msg_queue_push() defers an off-main caller before touching
# anything: the worker's message waits under the message queue lock
# for the main thread's iterate to replay it. Its widget, font,
# accessibility and settings work therefore happens on the main
# thread for every caller.
@boundary runloop_msg_queue_push

# The shader backends' per-frame sensor uniforms: the read side of a
# seqlock whose only writer is input_driver_poll() on the main thread
# (nine floats published as bits behind sensor_snap_seq). The getter
# call inside the helper is how it reaches the atomics, not a live
# state read.
input_driver_read_sensor_snapshot|input_state_get_ptr
# Release-store of the atomic shader-demand latch (shader_uses_sensors),
# read with acquire by the poll's demand check. A latch write, not a
# state read.
input_driver_set_shader_uses_sensors|input_state_get_ptr
# video_thread_swap_count: a documented cross-thread accessor. The
# video thread's own writes to swap_count happen under thr->lock in
# the wrapper's frame handler; this reader returns the field directly
# when called on the video thread (or with no wrapper), and takes the
# same thr->lock otherwise. The getter call is how it reaches the
# field; both sides are ordered by the lock.
video_thread_swap_count|video_state_get_ptr

# The widgets/menu/font/display-text stack, verified for the
# frame-context roots. Two safety arguments cover all five pairs:
#
# Batch state: gfx_display's quad batch (batch_* fields) is filled,
# flushed and latched only on the thread that renders - the video
# thread under the wrapper - so the flush/latch calls these callees
# make through disp_get_ptr touch thread-confined state. The
# main-written scalars on the same struct (framebuf dims, header
# height) are not read by these callees; menu drivers take their
# dimensions from their own snapshots per the menu sweep.
font_driver_render_msg|disp_get_ptr
menu_driver_frame|disp_get_ptr
gfx_widgets_frame|disp_get_ptr
#
# Vtable dispatch: the video_state reads are current_video, poke and
# data - the driver call pointers - which change only at video
# init/free, and a frame cannot execute across either (the wrapper
# joins and frees through blocking commands). Reading them to
# dispatch is reading init-stable pointers.
gfx_widgets_frame|video_state_get_ptr
gfx_display_draw_text|video_state_get_ptr
